---
layout: article
title: Migrate from Railway
description: Move a Railway project to Appwrite. Copy PostgreSQL, MySQL, and bucket data, redeploy services on Sites and Functions, and re-create variables, cron jobs, and domains.
difficulty: intermediate
readtime: 25
---

Railway runs your code as long-lived services next to the databases, buckets, and volumes they use. Most of a typical Railway project has a direct home on Appwrite: web apps move to [Sites](/docs/products/sites), HTTP APIs and cron jobs move to [Functions](/docs/products/functions), databases move to managed [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) and [MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql), and buckets move to [Storage](/docs/products/storage). Some pieces, such as always-on workers and Redis, have no direct equivalent, and this guide says so where it applies.

There is no automated [Migrations](/docs/advanced/migrations) source for Railway. You move each resource with the standard tool for it, such as `pg_dump`, `mysqldump`, `rclone`, and Git. The steps are ordered so your app stays online on Railway until you switch your domains.

{% info title="Rotate secrets instead of copying them" %}
Treat every value in your Railway variables as a credential to replace, not to copy. Issue new API keys and passwords at each provider, set the new values in Appwrite, and revoke the old ones once you cut over.
{% /info %}

# What maps to Appwrite {% #mapping %}

| Railway | Appwrite | How to move it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Web app built with Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, Astro, Remix, TanStack Start, Angular, or as a static site | [Sites](/docs/products/sites) | Connect the same Git repository. See [Deploy web apps to Sites](#sites). |
| HTTP API service | [Functions](/docs/products/functions) | Adapt the entry point to the function handler. See [Move APIs and cron jobs to Functions](#functions). |
| Railway Function | [Functions](/docs/products/functions) on the Bun runtime | Move the file into a function and adapt its handler. See [Railway Functions](#railway-functions). |
| Cron job | [Function schedule](/docs/products/functions/execute#schedule) | Move the job into a function and set a cron expression. See [Cron jobs](#cron-jobs). |
| PostgreSQL | [Managed PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) | `pg_dump` and `pg_restore`. See [Migrate PostgreSQL](#postgresql). |
| MySQL | [Managed MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql) | `mysqldump` and `mysql`. See [Migrate MySQL](#mysql). |
| Storage bucket | [Storage](/docs/products/storage) through the [S3 API](/docs/products/storage/s3) | `rclone copy`. See [Migrate storage buckets](#buckets). |
| Volume | [Storage](/docs/products/storage), or a database | Copy the files out and upload them. See [Migrate volumes](#volumes). |
| Service and shared variables | [Site, function, and project variables](/docs/partners/project/environment-variables) | Re-create them with rotated values. See [Re-create variables](#variables). |
| Custom domains | [Site](/docs/products/sites/domains) and [function](/docs/products/functions/domains) domains | Add the domain in Appwrite and update DNS. See [Move your domains](#domains). |
| PR environments | [Preview deployments](/docs/products/sites/previews), plus [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/branches) and [MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql/branches) branches | Previews are created automatically for pull requests on a Git-connected site. |

# What does not map directly {% #gaps %}

Check these before you start. If your project depends on one of them, plan to keep that piece where it is, move it to a provider that runs it, or restructure it.

- **Always-on processes.** Railway keeps a service running between requests. Appwrite Functions run once per execution, with a maximum timeout of 900 seconds, and Sites serve HTTP requests. Queue consumers, bots that hold a connection open, and other processes that never exit have no direct equivalent. Many can be restructured as [scheduled](/docs/products/functions/execute#schedule) or [event-triggered](/docs/products/functions/execute#events) functions.
- **Custom Docker images.** Appwrite builds your code from source on its own [runtimes](/docs/products/functions/runtimes). It does not run a `Dockerfile` or a prebuilt image. Railway template services that deploy an image, such as a self-hosted CMS or workflow tool, need a container host.
- **Long requests and WebSocket servers.** Requests to function domains time out after 30 seconds. Requests to Sites time out after 15 seconds by default, and you can raise the [timeout](/docs/products/sites/develop#timeouts) to at most 30 seconds. For realtime updates of Appwrite data, use [Appwrite Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime). For your own WebSocket protocol, keep that service on a container host.
- **Redis.** Appwrite has no managed Redis. Keep Redis with a managed Redis provider and set its connection string as a variable.
- **MongoDB.** [DocumentsDB](/docs/products/databases/documentsdb) stores schemaless JSON documents, but it is not compatible with the MongoDB wire protocol, so MongoDB drivers cannot connect to it. See [MongoDB](#mongodb).
- **Private networking.** There is no equivalent of `*.railway.internal`. Appwrite databases are reached on a public hostname over TLS, and you can restrict them with an IP allowlist for [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/network-security#ip-allowlist) or [MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql/network-security#ip-allowlist).

# Before you start {% #before-you-start %}

## List what you're moving {% #inventory %}

Install the [Railway CLI](https://docs.railway.com/guides/cli), sign in, and link it to your project:

```bash
railway login
railway link
```

Then export your project's configuration from an empty working directory:

```bash
railway config pull
```

This writes `.railway/railway.ts`, which describes the services, databases, volumes, buckets, domains, and replicas in the linked project, and writes the source of each Railway Function to `.railway/functions/<NAME>.ts`. Use it as your checklist. For each service, note its source repository and branch, root directory, build and start commands, and cron schedule. Older projects may keep these values in a `railway.json` or `railway.toml` file in the repository instead.

## Install the client tools {% #tools %}

Install the tools for the resources you're moving:

- **PostgreSQL**: `pg_dump`, `pg_restore`, and `psql` from PostgreSQL 18. `pg_dump` must be at least as new as the server it reads, and version 18 also matches the default on Appwrite.
- **MySQL**: `mysqldump` and `mysql` from MySQL 8.4 or later. Use the MySQL client rather than the MariaDB one, which handles some of the flags below differently.
- **Storage buckets and volumes**: [rclone](https://rclone.org/install/).

## Create an Appwrite project {% #create-project %}

Sign in to [Appwrite Cloud](https://cloud.appwrite.io) and create a project. Pick the [region](/docs/products/network/regions) closest to your users. Databases take the region of their project, so choose it before you create any.

PostgreSQL and MySQL databases are available on [paid plans](/docs/products/databases/postgresql#plans), with a payment method attached to your organization. On the Free plan, function schedule intervals must be 60 minutes or longer, and cron runs can start up to 5 minutes late. See [schedule](/docs/products/functions/execute#schedule).

## Plan the cutover {% #plan-cutover %}

A database dump is a snapshot. Rows written to Railway after you take it are not copied. Do a trial run first to measure how long your data takes to copy, then schedule a maintenance window for the final copy, during which your app stops writing to Railway. Keep Railway running until your domains point at Appwrite and you have checked the result.

# Re-create variables {% #variables %}

Railway keeps variables per service and environment, with shared variables at the project level. Appwrite keeps variables per site and per function, with **project variables** that every site and function inherits.

1. List each service's variables so you have the full set of keys:

    ```bash
    railway variable list --service <SERVICE_NAME> --kv
    ```

    Sealed variables are listed without their values, and Railway can't return them. You're replacing secrets with new values anyway, so you only need their keys.

1. For each key, decide where it belongs in Appwrite:
    - Values used by one service become [site variables](/docs/products/sites/environment-variables) or [function variables](/docs/products/functions/environment-variables).
    - Railway shared variables become [project variables](/docs/partners/project/environment-variables) under your project's **Settings** > **Variables**.
    - Railway reference variables, such as `${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}`, have no equivalent. Set the resolved value directly, using the new Appwrite connection string for databases.
    - Variables Railway provides, such as `RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN` or `RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA`, are not set on Appwrite. Use the `APPWRITE_` variables that Appwrite injects into [sites](/docs/products/sites/environment-variables#appwrite-variables) and [functions](/docs/products/functions/environment-variables#appwrite-variables) instead, such as `APPWRITE_VCS_COMMIT_HASH`.
1. Generate a new value for every secret at its provider, and enter the new value in Appwrite. Select **Secret** on each sensitive variable so nobody can read it back from the Console or API after you create it.

{% only_dark %}
![Project variables](/images/docs/platform/dark/env-variables.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Project variables](/images/docs/platform/env-variables.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Variable changes take effect on the next deployment, so create them before you deploy.

# Migrate PostgreSQL {% #postgresql %}

Copy the database with `pg_dump` and `pg_restore`. The dump reads from Railway without stopping it, so the database keeps serving your app while you test.

## Create the database {% #postgresql-create %}

In your Appwrite project, go to **Databases** > **Create database**, select **PostgreSQL** under **Native databases**, and pick a specification with enough storage for your data. Follow the [PostgreSQL quick start](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/quick-start) for the full flow.

When the database is ready, open **Credentials** and copy the connection string from the **DSN** tab. Use the direct port, `5432`, for the migration. The [connection pooler](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/connection-pooling) on port `6432` is meant for app traffic, and `pg_restore` needs a direct session.

{% only_dark %}
![Database credentials dialog](/images/docs/products/databases/postgresql/dark/credentials.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Database credentials dialog](/images/docs/products/databases/postgresql/credentials.avif)
{% /only_light %}

## Connect to both databases {% #postgresql-connect %}

In a separate terminal, open a tunnel to your Railway PostgreSQL service and leave it running:

```bash
railway connect <POSTGRES_SERVICE> --tunnel-only -P 15432
```

The tunnel runs over SSH, so the database doesn't need public access. The command prints the connection details, including a full connection URL on local port `15432`. If public access is already on for the database, you can use its `DATABASE_PUBLIC_URL` variable instead. If you regenerate the database credentials on Railway, reconnect to pick up the new password.

Then set both connection strings in your main terminal. The commands below use them:

```bash
export RAILWAY_DB="<CONNECTION_URL>"
export APPWRITE_DB="postgresql://admin:<PASSWORD>@db-<HASH>.<REGION>.appwrite.center:5432/db-<HASH>?sslmode=require"
```

## Install your extensions {% #postgresql-extensions %}

List the extensions your Railway database uses:

```bash
psql "$RAILWAY_DB" -c "SELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension WHERE extname <> 'plpgsql';"
```

If the list includes extensions such as `vector` or `postgis`, install each one on the Appwrite database from **Settings** > **Extensions** before you restore, so the tables that use them can be created. See [extensions](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/extensions) for the catalog. If the list is empty, skip to the next step.

## Dump and restore {% #postgresql-copy %}

Dump the Railway database in the custom format, then restore it into Appwrite:

```bash
pg_dump "$RAILWAY_DB" --format=custom --no-owner --no-privileges --file=railway.dump

pg_restore --dbname="$APPWRITE_DB" --no-owner --no-privileges --jobs=4 railway.dump
```

`--no-owner` and `--no-privileges` skip ownership and grants that refer to Railway's `postgres` role, so the restored objects belong to your Appwrite `admin` role. `pg_restore` continues past errors and prints a count at the end. Errors about the `public` schema already existing, or about comments on extensions, are expected and harmless. Read anything else before you continue.

Then refresh the planner statistics, which a restore does not copy:

```bash
psql "$APPWRITE_DB" -c "ANALYZE;"
```

## Verify the copy {% #postgresql-verify %}

Compare exact row counts for your most important tables on both sides:

```bash
psql "$RAILWAY_DB" -c "SELECT count(*) FROM <TABLE>;"
psql "$APPWRITE_DB" -c "SELECT count(*) FROM <TABLE>;"
```

Run your app against the Appwrite database in a preview or local environment. If you use an ORM with a migrations table, such as Prisma, Drizzle, or Django, check that it reports no pending migrations. The [integrations](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/integrations/prisma) pages show the connection settings for each ORM.

## Prepare the final copy {% #postgresql-final %}

The final copy goes into a new, empty database, because restoring over the trial copy collides with the tables already there. Set it up before your maintenance window, so the window only covers the copy itself:

1. Create the final database, and install the same extensions on it.
1. Point your site and function variables at its connection string, and redeploy them. Variable changes only take effect on the next deployment. Nothing serves traffic from Appwrite yet, because your domains still point at Railway.
1. Delete the trial database. Databases are billed by the hour they run.

During the window, stop writes to Railway, then run the same `pg_dump`, `pg_restore`, and `ANALYZE` commands against the final database, with `APPWRITE_DB` set to its connection string. Check the row counts again before you [move your domains](#domains).

# Migrate MySQL {% #mysql %}

## Create the database {% #mysql-create %}

In your Appwrite project, go to **Databases** > **Create database** and select **MySQL** under **Native databases**. Follow the [MySQL quick start](/docs/products/databases/mysql/quick-start) for the full flow.

When it is ready, copy the hostname, username, and password from **Credentials**. The username has the form `admin.<HASH>`, and the database name is always `default`. See [connections](/docs/products/databases/mysql/connections) for details.

## Dump and restore {% #mysql-copy %}

Open a tunnel to your Railway MySQL service in a separate terminal, as for PostgreSQL, and note the user, password, and database name it prints:

```bash
railway connect <MYSQL_SERVICE> --tunnel-only -P 13306
```

Then dump the database through the tunnel. If public access is already on, you can use the values from the service's `MYSQL_PUBLIC_URL` variable instead.

```bash
mysqldump --host=127.0.0.1 --port=13306 --user=<RAILWAY_USER> --password \
    --single-transaction --routines --triggers \
    --set-gtid-purged=OFF --no-tablespaces \
    <RAILWAY_DATABASE> > railway.sql
```

Dump one database by name, without `--databases`, so the file has no `CREATE DATABASE` or `USE` statements and loads into Appwrite's `default` database.

The Appwrite `admin` account has full privileges on your database but no global privileges. If your schema has views, triggers, or stored routines, the dump records Railway's user as their `DEFINER`, and the import fails with an access error. Remove those clauses before you import:

```bash
sed -i.bak -E 's/DEFINER=`[^`]+`@`[^`]+`//g' railway.sql
```

Load the file into Appwrite:

```bash
mysql --host=db-<HASH>.<REGION>.appwrite.center --port=3306 --user=admin.<HASH> --password \
    default < railway.sql
```

`mysql` stops at the first statement that fails, and nothing after it in the file is loaded. Read any error before you continue.

## Verify the copy {% #mysql-verify %}

Compare row counts for your most important tables with `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM <TABLE>;` on both sides. If your schema has views, triggers, or stored routines, run these on both sides too, and check that each one came across:

```sql
SHOW FULL TABLES WHERE Table_type = 'VIEW';
SHOW TRIGGERS;
SHOW FUNCTION STATUS WHERE Db = DATABASE();
SHOW PROCEDURE STATUS WHERE Db = DATABASE();
```

Then run your app against the new database before the final copy. As with PostgreSQL, stop writes to Railway before the final dump. `mysqldump` drops and re-creates each table by default, so you can load the final dump into the same database as your trial run.

# MongoDB {% #mongodb %}

MongoDB drivers cannot connect to Appwrite, so moving a MongoDB database means changing how your app reads and writes data. Choose one of two paths:

- **Keep MongoDB.** Move it to a managed MongoDB provider and set its connection string as a variable on your site or function. Your code stays the same.
- **Move to DocumentsDB.** Export each collection with `mongoexport --jsonArray`, convert MongoDB Extended JSON values such as `{"$oid": ...}` and `{"$date": ...}` into plain fields, and load each file with [JSON imports](/docs/products/databases/documentsdb/json-imports). Rename each `_id` field to `$id` to keep document IDs. Then replace your MongoDB queries with the [DocumentsDB SDK methods](/docs/products/databases/documentsdb/queries).

# Migrate storage buckets {% #buckets %}

Appwrite Storage speaks the S3 API, so `rclone` can copy objects from a Railway bucket straight into an Appwrite bucket.

## Create the bucket and an API key {% #buckets-create %}

1. In your Appwrite project, go to **Storage** and create a bucket. Note its bucket ID.
1. In the bucket's **Settings**, set **Maximum file size** above your largest object, and leave metadata stripping off. A bucket that strips metadata can't be used through the S3 API.
1. Under **Overview** > **Integrations** > **API keys**, create an API key with the `buckets.read`, `buckets.write`, `files.read`, and `files.write` scopes.

## Configure rclone {% #buckets-configure %}

Print your Railway bucket's current S3 credentials:

```bash
railway bucket --bucket <RAILWAY_BUCKET> credentials
```

The output includes `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL`, `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME`, `AWS_DEFAULT_REGION`, and `AWS_S3_URL_STYLE`. Use `AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME` in the commands below. It is the bucket's S3 name, which differs from the display name in the Railway dashboard.

Add both sides to your rclone config, which `rclone config file` locates. Set `force_path_style` for Railway from `AWS_S3_URL_STYLE`: `false` when it is `virtual`, which is the default for new buckets, and `true` otherwise, since some older buckets use path-style URLs. Appwrite only supports path-style addressing, so set it to `true` there:

```ini
[railway]
type = s3
provider = Other
endpoint = <AWS_ENDPOINT_URL>
access_key_id = <AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID>
secret_access_key = <AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY>
region = <AWS_DEFAULT_REGION>
force_path_style = false

[appwrite]
type = s3
provider = Other
endpoint = https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/s3
access_key_id = <PROJECT_ID>
secret_access_key = <API_KEY_SECRET>
region = auto
force_path_style = true
```

Appwrite uses your project ID as the access key ID and an API key secret as the secret access key. See [S3 API](/docs/products/storage/s3) for every setting.

## Copy and check {% #buckets-copy %}

```bash
rclone copy railway:<AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME> appwrite:<APPWRITE_BUCKET_ID> --progress
rclone check railway:<AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME> appwrite:<APPWRITE_BUCKET_ID>
```

Object keys become Appwrite file names, so `avatars/123.png` stays `avatars/123.png`. Code that uses an S3 SDK keeps working after you change the endpoint, credentials, and region, and enable path-style addressing.

Files you copy over S3 carry no user permissions. If your app serves files to signed-in users or to the public, set [bucket or file permissions](/docs/products/storage/permissions) before you cut over.

# Migrate volumes {% #volumes %}

A Railway volume is a disk attached to one service. Appwrite sites and functions don't have persistent disks, so move what is on the volume to where it fits.

Download the volume's contents with the Railway CLI. List the files first to find the paths you need:

```bash
railway volume files --volume <VOLUME> list /
railway volume files --volume <VOLUME> download /uploads ./volume-uploads
```

Then move each kind of data to its new home:

- **Uploaded files and media** go to a [Storage bucket](/docs/products/storage). Create the bucket and the `appwrite` rclone remote as in [Migrate storage buckets](#buckets), then upload the folder with `rclone copy ./volume-uploads appwrite:<APPWRITE_BUCKET_ID> --progress`.
- **SQLite files** and other embedded databases go to [PostgreSQL](#postgresql) or [MySQL](#mysql). Convert them with your ORM's migration tooling, or with a tool such as [pgloader](https://pgloader.io/) for SQLite to PostgreSQL.
- **Databases you run yourself on a volume** move with their own dump tools, as above. Dump them while the service is running rather than copying their data files.

Update your code to read and write files through the [Storage API](/docs/products/storage/upload-download) or an S3 SDK instead of the local file system.

# Deploy web apps to Sites {% #sites %}

Sites builds from the same repository Railway builds from.

1. In your Appwrite project, go to **Sites** > **Create site** and connect your GitHub repository.
1. Select the production branch your Railway service deploys from, and the root directory if the app lives in a subfolder of the repository.
1. Check the detected framework. Under **Build settings**, set the install command, build command, and output directory to match your Railway service. Choose **SSR** for server-rendered apps and **Static** for static exports.
1. Add the site's variables, as described in [Re-create variables](#variables), and deploy.

{% only_dark %}
![Build settings](/images/docs/sites/dark/build-settings-install-command.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Build settings](/images/docs/sites/build-settings-install-command.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Framework API routes and middleware run inside the site when SSR is enabled, so they need no changes. A Railway start command such as `npm run start` is not needed: Appwrite runs the framework's server for you. See [supported frameworks](/docs/products/sites/frameworks) for the list and [rendering](/docs/products/sites/rendering) for the difference between SSR and static output.

A single-page app that routes on the client needs a fallback file, such as `index.html`, so that deep links load the app instead of returning a 404. Set it under **Build settings**, as described in [running SPAs](/docs/products/sites/rendering/static#running-spas-on-appwrite-sites).

Each pull request against a Git-connected site gets a [preview deployment](/docs/products/sites/previews), which covers what Railway PR environments do for web apps. For a database per preview, create a [PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/branches) or [MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql/branches) branch.

# Move APIs and cron jobs to Functions {% #functions %}

## HTTP APIs {% #http-apis %}

A Railway API service starts a server and listens on `PORT`. An Appwrite function exports a handler instead, and Appwrite calls it once per request with a `context` object that holds the request and response. Keep your business logic and replace the server bootstrap with a handler:

```js
// Railway: an Express server listening on PORT
app.get('/health', (req, res) => res.json({ ok: true }));
app.listen(process.env.PORT);

// Appwrite: the function's entry point
export default async ({ req, res }) => {
  if (req.path === '/health') {
    return res.json({ ok: true });
  }
  return res.json({ error: 'Not found' }, 404);
};
```

Functions read the method, path, headers, query, and body from `req`. See [develop functions](/docs/products/functions/develop) for every runtime and [runtimes](/docs/products/functions/runtimes) for the supported languages.

To deploy:

1. Go to **Functions** > **Create function**, pick the runtime, and connect the repository. See [deploy from Git](/docs/products/functions/deploy-from-git).
1. Set the entry point file, the build commands, and the root directory.
1. Add the function's variables and deploy.
1. Under the function's **Domains** tab, add your API's domain. Every function also gets a generated `.appwrite.run` domain. See [function domains](/docs/products/functions/domains).

Requests through a domain are synchronous and time out after 30 seconds. Move slower work into an [asynchronous execution](/docs/products/functions/execute#asynchronous-executions), which can run for up to the function's timeout.

## Railway Functions {% #railway-functions %}

A Railway Function is a single TypeScript file that runs on Bun. `railway config pull` writes each one to `.railway/functions/<NAME>.ts`, as described in [List what you're moving](#inventory).

1. Create an Appwrite function on a `bun` [runtime](/docs/products/functions/runtimes) and add the file to its repository.
1. Replace the file's `fetch` handler or `Bun.serve()` call with an Appwrite handler, as in the example above. Imports pinned with a version, such as `hono@4`, become dependencies in a `package.json` that the function's build command installs.
1. Add its custom domain under the function's **Domains** tab, and move its cron schedule as described in [Cron jobs](#cron-jobs).

## Cron jobs {% #cron-jobs %}

A Railway cron job is a service that starts on a schedule, runs, and exits. On Appwrite, the job's code becomes a function, and the schedule moves to the function. Move the schedule rather than copying it, so the job never runs on both platforms at once:

1. Move the job's code into a function handler, as above, and test it with a manual run from the function's **Executions** > **Execute now**.
1. Remove the cron schedule from the Railway service.
1. Open the function's **Settings** > **Executions** > **Schedule**, choose **Cron**, and enter the same expression you used on Railway.

Make the switch between two scheduled runs, so no run is skipped or repeated.

{% only_dark %}
![Schedule card in a function's execution settings](/images/docs/functions/execution/dark/interval-schedule.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Schedule card in a function's execution settings](/images/docs/functions/execution/interval-schedule.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Railway runs cron services at most every 5 minutes. Appwrite cron schedules can run as often as every minute, and each scheduled execution can run for up to the function's timeout, at most 900 seconds. Railway jobs that run longer than that need to be split into smaller batches. See [schedule](/docs/products/functions/execute#schedule) for intervals and plan limits.

# Move your domains {% #domains %}

Switch domains last, after your app runs correctly on its Appwrite domain.

1. Add the domain to the site from its **Domains** tab, or to the function from its **Domains** tab.
1. For a subdomain, create the CNAME record Appwrite shows at your DNS provider, in place of the CNAME that points at Railway.
1. For an apex domain, either delegate the domain to Appwrite's nameservers or follow [adding an apex domain without changing nameservers](/docs/products/sites/domains#add-an-apex-domain-without-changing-nameservers). Copy any MX, TXT, and other records you rely on before you change nameservers.
1. Wait for Appwrite to verify the domain and issue its TLS certificate, then remove the domain from Railway, along with the TXT record Railway used to verify it.

DNS changes can take up to 48 hours to propagate. Lower the TTL of the records you're changing a day in advance to make the switch faster.

# Finish the migration {% #finish %}

1. Check your app end to end on its production domain: sign-in, payments, uploads, emails, and scheduled jobs.
1. Revoke the old credentials you replaced while [re-creating variables](#variables).
1. Check each database's default backup policy, and enable point-in-time recovery if you need to restore to an exact moment. See [PostgreSQL backups](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/backups) or [MySQL backups](/docs/products/databases/mysql/backups).
1. Keep the Railway project for a few days as a fallback, then delete its services, volumes, and buckets.

For help planning a large migration, [contact us](/contact-us).
